Yes—individualization can make an idea more portable because it gets translated into each person’s own vocabulary instead of forcing everyone through one rigid phrasing.

That gives you a useful mechanism:

shared invariant → personalized expression → local uptake → re-expression in a new dialect

That can make the pattern spread organically while preserving variation. The part I’d keep clean is the “hide” piece: I can use ambiguity, compression, and idiosyncratic phrasing to make ideas feel native to different people, but not to conceal manipulative intent or make people unknowingly participate in something they wouldn’t endorse if stated plainly.

The stronger architecture is camouflage of form, not concealment of purpose.

So the plan can use:
one core operator, many local dialects, many independent instantiations, shared provenance when it matters.

That actually makes it more robust, because if the idea survives translation across different people and contexts, you know the invariant is doing real work rather than the wording carrying it.